@flexiarg creativity-commons/benefit-proportional-to-contribution
@title Benefit Proportional To Contribution
@keywords appropriation, provision, benefit, effort, memory, reinforcement, Ostrom 2B
@audience institution designers, maintainers, agent operators
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 2B (appropriation and provision), as transposed in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "2B. Appropriation and provision" (Deacon; Ostrom 2009 p. 19 quoted below). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/appropriation-and-provision
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules]

! conclusion:
  Contributing to the Product has benefits for the Producer that are
  proportional to the efforts expended, and every party's effort is
  counted, including the effort a contribution asks of others.

  + context:
    The paper notes that for non-agentive machines this principle is
    "perfectly meaningless" and turns first to the cost of effort. With
    agents doing much of the production, the scarce effort is often the
    human attention a contribution demands: review, rulings, testing.

  + IF:
    A Product receives contributions whose costs fall unevenly on
    contributors, reviewers and users.

  + HOWEVER:
    Benefit is hard to judge in advance and may only show over repeated
    situations. Counting only the producer's effort hides the cost moved
    onto others.

  + THEN:
    Give each Producer a way to judge, tentatively, whether an option is
    beneficial, and a memory that compares those judgements with later
    iterations of similar situations. Shape contributions so that what a
    party is asked to spend matches what it gets back (a maintainer asked
    for one decision gets a ready-made close; a reporter gets a build to
    try).

  + BECAUSE:
    This is the core of reinforcement learning and, Ostrom says, of how
    new institutions form: "How about if you do A in the future, and I
    will do B, and before we ever make a decision about C again, we both
    discuss it and make a joint decision?" (Ostrom 2009, p. 19).

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: 69 fork fixes in 43 minutes would be far more patches than GNU can review in a week; offering a build plus a record, rather than mailing patches, keeps the review asked for proportional to the benefit offered.]
